ForgeData.register("englit",{"ENG-TERM-1":{"label":"Literary & Linguistic Terminology","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"ENG-01","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks?","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The imagery is a motif that appears throughout the text.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENG-02","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","B":"Soliloquy — the character thinks aloud for the audience's benefit.","C":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator.","D":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENG-03","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis?","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Asyndeton — the deliberate omission of conjunctions to speed up the rhythm.","C":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENG-04","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding?","options":{"A":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENG-05","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly?","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","B":"Anticlimax — the story ends disappointingly.","C":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENG-06","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct?","options":{"A":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","B":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","C":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting.","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENG-07","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition?","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"Register means accent and dialect — the geographical variety of language used.","C":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","D":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENG-08","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write?","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student is correct — repetition for emphasis is an acceptable AO2 observation.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"ENG-TERM-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-001","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-003","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-005","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-007","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-009","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost?","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-011","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4?","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-013","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here?","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-015","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening?","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-017","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-019","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-021","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"a student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-023","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-025","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-027","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-029","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-031","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-033","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (exam practice variant) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-035","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-037","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-039","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-041","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-043","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-045","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"a student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-047","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-049","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (alternative exam formulation) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-051","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (applied formulation) (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-053","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (comparison formulation) (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-055","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (second formulation) (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-057","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-059","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-061","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-063","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-065","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (alternative exam formulation) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-067","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (applied formulation) (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-069","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"a student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (comparison formulation) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-071","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (second formulation) (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-073","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (exam practice variant) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-075","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (another formulation) (revision variant)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-077","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (revision variant) (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-079","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (alternative wording) (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-081","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (alternative exam formulation) (applied formulation) (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-083","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (applied formulation) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-085","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (comparison formulation) (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-087","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (second formulation) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-089","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (exam practice variant) (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-091","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (another formulation) (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-093","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"a student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (revision variant) (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-095","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (alternative wording) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-097","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-099","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-101","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-103","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-105","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-107","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-109","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-111","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-113","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks?","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-115","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis?","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? 'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-117","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"which response shows accurate understanding here? A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly?","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? 'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-119","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition?","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-121","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost?","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-123","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4?","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-125","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here?","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-127","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening?","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-129","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (comparison formulation) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-131","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-133","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-135","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which option is defensible when the context changes? Which claim is defensible when the context changes? A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-137","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-139","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"a student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-141","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-143","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-145","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-147","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (exam practice variant) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-149","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (revision variant) (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-151","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (alternative exam formulation) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-153","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-155","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-157","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-159","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-161","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-163","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"a student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-165","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-167","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-169","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost? (comparison formulation) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"The student should cite a secondary source when making contextual claims."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"They should move on from context and focus on language analysis instead."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-171","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","C":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-173","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"'Similarly' is fine — AO4 is met whenever two texts are mentioned in the same paragraph."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","D":"The comparison is valid — noting both poets use natural imagery meets AO4."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-175","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them.","D":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-177","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student identifies the following in a poem about war: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. They write: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.' Which response would earn more marks? (alternative exam formulation) (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","B":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","C":"The student should call it a metaphor, since the words create a vivid picture.","D":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Semantic field (also called lexical field): a group of words related in meaning that belong to the same conceptual domain. Identifying a semantic field is analytically stronger than saying 'imagery' because it shows the pattern of deliberate lexical choice across the text. 'Imagery' usually refers to figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) creating a mental picture — it requires a comparison or non-literal meaning. A list of war words is a semantic field, not imagery unless they function figuratively. AO2 rewards precision: 'semantic field of violence creates a cumulative effect of inevitability' > 'imagery of death makes it vivid'. Examiner reports consistently note that top responses name the pattern, not just the individual word.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'The writer uses the imagery of money — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to suggest the character's greed.' What should the student say instead?","options":{"A":"The writer uses a metaphor of money to suggest greed.","B":"The student should identify this as a semantic field — a cluster of lexis from the same conceptual domain. Naming it as 'semantic field of death/violence' is more precise and earns AO2 marks by showing the deliberate lexical patterning rather than treating each word in isolation.","C":"The writer creates a semantic field of finance and commerce — gold, coin, debt, worth, price — to reveal the character's preoccupation with monetary value, suggesting greed shapes their entire worldview.","D":"The writer uses personification of money to suggest greed."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-179","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student annotates: 'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once' as 'repetition for emphasis.' What is the more precise term and what does using it add to the analysis? (applied formulation) (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"Polysyndeton — the deliberate use of multiple coordinating conjunctions ('and...and...and') in close succession. It is more precise than 'repetition' because it identifies the specific syntactic pattern and its effect: slowing the pace to create a sense of accumulation, weight, or relentlessness — here, the storm elements pile up as if unstoppable.","C":"The student is correct — identifying the imagery is sufficient for AO1.","D":"Syndeton — the standard grammatical use of conjunctions, no different from normal prose."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"Polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions (and, or, but) beyond grammatical necessity — 'and this and that and the other'. Effect: accumulation, weight, breathlessness, relentlessness, the sense of things piling up or being catalogued. Biblical register. Contrast with asyndeton: deliberate omission of conjunctions — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'. Effect: speed, urgency, staccato impact, compression. Both are syntactic/structural choices, not lexical ones — the effect comes from sentence architecture, not word meaning. Common error: calling polysyndeton 'repetition' — technically accurate but analytically imprecise. AO2 marks come from naming the pattern, describing the syntactic effect, and connecting it to meaning: 'polysyndeton slows the rhythm so each element of the storm accumulates, overwhelming the reader as it overwhelms the landscape.'","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She ran and stumbled and fell and got up and ran again.' What is the technique and its effect?","options":{"A":"Repetition — 'and' is used multiple times for emphasis.","B":"Polysyndeton — the repeated 'and' connects each action without pause, creating a relentless, exhausting rhythm that mirrors the character's desperate physical and psychological state: she cannot stop even to punctuate her own escape.","C":"Anaphora — 'and' is repeated at the start of each clause.","D":"The simile makes the horror of war seem so extreme that even evil is sickened by it."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-181","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The poem ends with bathos — after describing the hero's great deeds, it ends with him eating toast.' Is the student using the term correctly? (comparison formulation) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","D":"No — bathos refers to a sustained low or humble style throughout, not a sudden descent."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"Bathos (from Greek: 'depth'): an abrupt, jarring descent from the elevated or serious to the trivial or mundane, producing a comic, deflating, or ironic effect. Originally described by Alexander Pope as an unintentional failure of sublimity; now used for deliberate comic deflation too. Key test: is the descent producing irony, comedy, or puncturing of pretension? If yes → bathos. If the mundane ending is simply realistic without comic intent → anticlimax or tonal contrast, not necessarily bathos. Examples: Austen's ironic social commentary often uses bathos to deflate pomposity; Eliot's Prufrock ('I have measured out my life with coffee spoons') uses bathos to contrast heroic aspiration with mundane reality. AO2 mark: name the device + describe the tonal descent + analyse why the writer chooses deflation at this moment (irony? subversion of genre expectations? comic relief?).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'She had survived the war, crossed three borders, rebuilt her life from nothing — and now she couldn't find her keys.' Is this bathos, anticlimax, or something else?","options":{"A":"This is potentially bathos if the lost keys produce ironic comedy or puncture heroic register, or anticlimax if the intent is to show ordinary life reasserting itself after trauma. The distinction depends on tone: comedy and deflation = bathos; melancholy domesticity = tonal anticlimax. Without the surrounding text, the term cannot be applied with confidence — AO2 requires contextual evidence of intent.","B":"Yes — any anticlimactic ending is bathos.","C":"Bathos — it descends from the serious to the trivial.","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-183","spec":"ENG-TERM-1","stem":"A student defines register as 'formal or informal language.' How would an examiner improve this definition? (second formulation) (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student is correct — deixis means pointing and that is the full definition.","C":"Register refers only to the vocabulary level — simple or complex words.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Register: the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Comprises three dimensions: (1) Field — the subject matter and specialist vocabulary it generates (legal register, medical register, sports commentary register); (2) Tenor — the relationship between participants and the degree of formality, intimacy, or authority it encodes (a letter to a friend vs to a judge — same writer, different tenor); (3) Mode — the channel of communication and its conventions (written, spoken, digital; planned vs spontaneous). Why it matters for AO2: register shifts within a text signal a change in purpose, relationship, or power. A politician shifting from formal to colloquial register is constructing accessibility; a character shifting from intimate to formal language is creating distance. AO2 mark: identify the register → describe its features (field, tenor, mode) → analyse what the register choice constructs about the relationship or power dynamic.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A politician's speech opens with 'My fellow citizens' then shifts to 'Look, the thing is, we've all been there, haven't we?' What does the register shift achieve?","options":{"A":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","B":"The shift from elevated civic register ('fellow citizens' — formal, distancing, constructing authority) to colloquial register ('look', 'the thing is', tag question 'haven't we?') performs a deliberate construction of democratic solidarity and accessibility. The tenor shifts from authority to peer — the politician positions themselves as one of the people, using the grammar of spoken conversation to simulate authenticity and intimacy.","C":"The politician uses informal language because they are nervous.","D":"The register is inconsistent — a sign of poor stylistic control."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-1","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-1","aqa-a-engll-3-1"]}]},"ENG-TECH-1":{"label":"Exam Technique & AO Application","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"ENG-09","stem":"A student writes: 'Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in 1606, shortly after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, when James I was on the throne and feared witchcraft.' Where is the AO3 mark lost?","options":{"A":"The context is provided as background information rather than being connected to how it shapes meaning in the text. AO3 requires the student to show how context actively inflects their reading of a specific moment — not to supply historical wallpaper. The examiner cannot award AO3 marks for contextual information that sits beside the analysis without doing any analytical work.","B":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603.","C":"The student has included too much context — one fact is sufficient.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"AO3 (context) is the most commonly misapplied assessment objective in English Literature. Examiners describe two modes of contextual writing: (1) Context as wallpaper — historical or biographical information stated as fact, disconnected from analysis of the text. Earns zero or minimal AO3 marks. (2) Context as lens — the contextual information is used to show how it shapes the meaning of a specific textual moment. Earns full AO3 marks. The test: can you remove the contextual sentence without damaging the analysis? If yes, it's wallpaper. Good AO3: 'The witches' prophecies would have struck a Jacobean audience — presided over by a king who published Daemonologie (1597) — as a direct invocation of royal paranoia, positioning Macbeth's susceptibility to supernatural manipulation as a politically charged failure of monarchical reason.' The context explains why the moment reads differently; it is not decorative.","reforge":{"stem":"A student writes about The Handmaid's Tale: 'Atwood wrote this in 1985, during the Reagan era, when there was a rise in religious conservatism in America.' How should they continue to earn AO3 marks?","options":{"A":"They should add more historical detail about the Reagan administration.","B":"They have already earned AO3 marks by providing the historical context.","C":"They should connect the context directly to a specific moment in the text — for example: 'This context inflects the Republic of Gilead's theocratic control of female bodies not as dystopian fantasy but as an extrapolation of policies already normalising in 1980s America, positioning Offred's narrative as a warning directed at a contemporary reader rather than a distant future.'","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENG-10","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically?","options":{"A":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","B":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise.","B":"The visit shows that Joe and Pip have grown apart because Pip has become richer.","C":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENG-11","stem":"A student writes: 'Plath uses the metaphor of the bell jar — quote: \"I felt myself enclosed in a glass bell jar\" — which means she feels trapped.' What does the student need to add to reach Band 4?","options":{"A":"A longer quotation — the student needs to quote more of the text.","B":"A secondary critic's view — the student needs to reference a named scholar.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-TONE-METAPHOR","scaffold":"The quote-drop-paraphrase pattern is the most common reason students plateau at Band 2-3: they identify a technique, embed a quotation, then restate what the quotation says in different words ('which means she feels trapped' = paraphrase of 'enclosed in a glass bell jar'). Band 4 analysis requires: (1) Unpacking the specific semantic, structural, or sonic properties of the language — what is it about THIS word or phrase that creates the effect? (2) Exploring multiple implications or resonances of the image — what does glass carry as a signifier? What does bell jar specifically evoke beyond 'enclosure'? (3) Connecting the technique to theme, characterisation, narrative structure, or context. Examiner guidance: 'quotation followed by paraphrase = no AO2 marks'. AO2 marks are awarded for analysis of how the language creates meaning, not for restating what it describes.","reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Owen uses the simile \"like a devil's sick of sin\" to describe the gas victim — this shows the man is in terrible pain and looks frightening.' What analysis would earn Band 4?","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The student should quote more of the poem to support their point.","C":"The student should find a critic who has written about this simile.","D":"The student should explain that Owen was a soldier who experienced gas attacks and describe when he served, where he was stationed, how his military service affected his health and which biographical sources record those events. They should also add a chronology of his postings, publication history and hospital treatment, then use those facts as the main evidence for the poem. That information may provide historical context, but it does not analyse the simile's paradox, imagery, moral inversion or the way the comparison presents the battlefield as a source of suffering beyond ordinary human endurance."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENG-12","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks?","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENG-13","stem":"A student is writing a comparative essay and uses the phrase 'Similarly, the writer of Text B also shows that...' to link two texts. Why might an examiner be cautious about awarding AO4 marks here?","options":{"A":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should use 'likewise' or 'in the same way' instead of 'similarly'.","D":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"AO4 (connections across texts) is not met by writing about two texts alternately or by using comparative connectives. Examiners describe the failure mode as 'tennis match comparison' — the student hits Text A, then Text B, then Text A, without establishing why the connection matters. AO4 at the top band requires: (1) The connection reveals something about both texts — it is not decorative but analytical; (2) The student can articulate what the comparison illuminates — a shared theme treated differently, a contrast in form that reflects different contexts, a dialogue between texts; (3) The comparison shapes the student's interpretation of each text, not just notes similarities. Practical rule: after every comparative move, ask 'so what does this connection tell us?' If the answer is only 'they both deal with loss', the AO4 has not been earned. If the answer is 'both texts construct grief through fragmented syntax, but Heaney's fragments accumulate while Duffy's remain suspended — suggesting different relationships to the possibility of recovery', that is AO4.","reforge":{"stem":"A student compares two poems about memory and writes: 'Poem A uses imagery of water to represent memory. Similarly, Poem B also uses natural imagery.' What is wrong with this comparison and how should it be improved?","options":{"A":"The comparison is wrong — the poems are actually very different.","B":"The student should use 'likewise' instead of 'similarly'.","C":"The comparison notes surface similarity (both use natural imagery) without establishing what the connection reveals. Improved: 'Both poets use natural imagery to figure memory's instability — but where Poem A's water imagery emphasises memory's fluidity and erosion (the past literally dissolving), Poem B's imagery of roots and soil constructs memory as something buried but persistent, potentially recoverable. The contrast suggests different relationships to loss: one accepts dissolution, the other insists on retrieval.'","D":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENG-14","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing?","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","C":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'I think the lighthouse represents hope because it guides ships to safety.'","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENG-15","stem":"A student is asked to write about language in an unseen prose extract for AQA English Language & Literature. They begin: 'This extract uses many different language techniques including metaphor, alliteration, and personification.' What is wrong with this opening?","options":{"A":"The student has identified the wrong techniques — they should focus on syntax.","B":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","C":"The opening catalogues techniques without analysis — it tells the examiner what devices are present rather than demonstrating any understanding of how they create meaning. A list of techniques is not analysis. The student should open with an analytical claim about the extract's overall effect or purpose, then use specific techniques as evidence for that claim.","D":"The student is correct to establish the techniques present before analysing them."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The technique-catalogue opening ('this text uses X, Y, and Z') is the single most common structural error in unseen language analysis. It signals to the examiner that the student is listing rather than analysing — they have spotted devices but haven't yet made a claim about what the text does. Strong unseen analysis starts with an interpretive claim: what is the extract's overall effect, purpose, or dominant impression? Then individual techniques are used as evidence for that claim. Structure: [Interpretive claim about the whole extract] → [First technique + specific example + how it constructs that effect] → [Second technique + how it develops or complicates the effect] → [Structural/form observation about how the extract is organised]. AQA 7707 mark scheme: 'methods identified without analysis of effect' = Band 1-2. 'Detailed analysis of how methods create meanings and effects' = Band 4-5.","reforge":{"stem":"Rewrite this opening to earn Band 4 marks: 'This extract uses personification of the sea, short sentences, and direct address to the reader.'","options":{"A":"'The extract constructs the sea as an active, predatory force through sustained personification — the ocean does not simply exist but watches, pursues, and threatens. This anthropomorphisation is intensified by the staccato rhythm of short declarative sentences, which enact the sudden violence of waves, and by the direct address ('you'), which implicates the reader in the speaker's vulnerability rather than positioning them as a safe observer.'","B":"'This extract is about the sea and uses many techniques to describe it effectively.'","C":"'The writer uses personification because it makes the sea seem alive and threatening.'","D":"Partially — bathos requires the descent to be deliberately comic or deflating, not merely anticlimactic. If the mundane ending undermines the heroic tone for ironic or comic effect, it is bathos. But if the mundane ending is simply realistic or melancholic — a hero returning to ordinary life after trauma — it may be deliberately bathetic in register but not in the classical rhetorical sense. The student needs to show the tonal descent is intentional and that it produces irony or comedy, not just surprise."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENG-16","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice?","options":{"A":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","B":"The student needs to compare the poem's form to other poems in the collection.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"ENG-TECH-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-002","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise?","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-004","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding?","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-006","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-008","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write?","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-010","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-012","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-014","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-016","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-018","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-020","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-022","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"a student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-024","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-026","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (alternative exam formulation) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-028","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-030","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-032","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-034","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-036","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-038","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-040","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-042","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-044","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (comparison formulation) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-046","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"a student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-048","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-050","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-052","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-054","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-056","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-058","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-060","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-062","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-064","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-066","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (alternative exam formulation) (revision variant)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-068","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (applied formulation) (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-070","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"a student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (comparison formulation) (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-072","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (second formulation) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-074","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (exam practice variant) (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-076","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (another formulation) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-078","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (revision variant) (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-080","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (alternative wording) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-082","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (alternative exam formulation) (revision variant) (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-084","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (applied formulation) (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-086","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (comparison formulation) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-088","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (second formulation) (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-090","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (exam practice variant) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-092","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (another formulation) (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-094","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"a student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (revision variant) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-096","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (alternative wording) (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-098","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-100","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-102","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-104","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-106","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-108","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-110","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-112","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-114","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise?","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? 'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-116","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding?","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-118","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"which response shows accurate understanding here? A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-120","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write?","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-122","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically?","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? 'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-124","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks?","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-126","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing?","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-128","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice?","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which response shows accurate understanding here? A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-130","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (alternative wording) (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-132","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (applied formulation) (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-134","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-136","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which option is defensible when the context changes? Which claim is defensible when the context changes? A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-138","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-140","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"an A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"'Similarly' asserts a parallel without demonstrating it — AO4 requires the student to show how the connection between texts illuminates meaning in both, not simply note that two things exist. If the connection is genuinely similar, the student must explain what the similarity reveals about a shared theme, context, or technique. If the student has forced a similarity where the texts actually diverge, using 'similarly' to avoid engaging with the contrast loses marks. AO4 is awarded for meaningful connections, not for the presence of comparative connectives.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-142","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-144","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-146","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-148","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-150","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (second formulation) (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-152","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-154","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (revision variant)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-156","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"The student needs to unpack the theological paradox of the simile: a devil who is sick of sin has exhausted the very substance of its identity — the comparison positions the victim beyond even hellish suffering, in a register that transcends pain into existential horror. The inversion (devil = the creature defined by sin, now repelled by it) enacts the war's moral inversion: the battlefield has created suffering so extreme it sickens the agent of evil. Owen implicates the reader ('you') who has been complicit in the lie of dulce et decorum est, making the simile an accusation as much as a description.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-158","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-160","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-162","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-164","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"a student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-166","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-168","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (another formulation) (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-170","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.' What AO failure does this demonstrate most critically? (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"AO2 failure — the student is narrating plot events (what happens) rather than analysing method (how the writer constructs meaning). No technique is identified, no language is analysed, and no sense of Fitzgerald's authorial choices emerges. The student treats the novel as a series of events that happened rather than as a constructed text where every choice is deliberate.","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"AO3 failure — the student has not mentioned any context.","D":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-AO2-METHOD","scaffold":"The single most consistent failure across A Level English essays — at every mark band — is narrating plot instead of analysing method. The distinction: plot narrative tells the examiner what happens; literary analysis shows how the writer constructs meaning through specific choices of language, form, and structure. Practical test: replace the student's name with a synopsis website. If the writing reads like a plot summary, it has not earned AO2 marks. The switch: instead of 'Gatsby throws parties', write 'Fitzgerald constructs Gatsby's extravagance through the spectacle of his parties — the catalogues of excess (names, foods, music, cars) create a surface of dazzling abundance that deliberately resists interiority, suggesting that Gatsby's identity is pure performance, a constructed spectacle designed for Daisy's gaze.' Every sentence should contain: a method or technique + a specific textual moment + an analysis of the effect or meaning constructed.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'Pip is embarrassed by Joe when he visits London. He is ashamed of his working-class background.' Rewrite this as literary analysis.","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"AO1 failure — the student is not using literary terminology.","C":"Dickens constructs Pip's class anxiety through his narrated response to Joe's visit — the retrospective narrator's voice carries both the original embarrassment and a guilt that the older Pip cannot quite suppress, creating an ironic doubling where the reader is invited to judge the young Pip even as the narrative voice attempts to excuse him. The awkward physicality of Joe's hat — repeatedly mentioned — becomes a synecdoche for working-class unease in a middle-class space.","D":"Pip's embarrassment shows he has changed as a character since the beginning of the novel."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-172","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"An A Level student is asked to 'explore the significance of the opening of the novel.' They write a detailed analysis of language and structure. What does 'explore' require that their response must include to access top marks? (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","B":"'Explore' requires the student to consider multiple possible readings or interpretations of the same textual moment, weighing them against each other rather than asserting a single meaning. It also invites consideration of what different readers might make of the opening — including the significance of the word 'significance' itself, which in AQA specifications requires engagement with AO2, AO3, AO4, and AO5.","C":"'Explore' is identical to 'analyse' — both mean examine the language closely.","D":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Command word precision matters at A Level. 'Explore' signals: consider multiple interpretations, not a single thesis. It invites the student to hold alternative readings in tension and evaluate their relative validity — this is where AO5 (personal response) lives. Contrast with 'analyse' (interrogate the specific methods and effects) and 'assess' or 'evaluate' (weigh evidence and reach a substantiated judgement). In AQA English Literature, 'significance' is a term with specific AO implications: it activates AO2 (how language creates meaning), AO3 (how context shapes reception), AO4 (connections to other texts or traditions), and AO5 (the student's own informed personal response). A response that only does language analysis on the opening but doesn't address why the opening is significant — what it establishes, what it invites, how it positions the reader — has not fully answered the question regardless of the quality of the AO2 work.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student asked to 'explore how Shakespeare presents power in Othello' writes a detailed close reading of Iago's language in Act 1. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The student needs to write about more scenes from the play.","B":"Register is broader than formality: it encompasses the variety of language appropriate to a specific social situation, purpose, and relationship between participants. It includes: field (subject matter vocabulary), tenor (the relationship between writer and reader — formal, intimate, authoritative), and mode (spoken, written, digital). A legal document and a medical report are both formal but belong to different registers. Register choices reveal power dynamics, social positioning, and the writer's construction of their relationship with the audience.","C":"The student needs to include a named critic's view on Iago.","D":"The student has analysed one character's use of language but 'explore' requires multiple perspectives on power — how Brabantio's racial power, Othello's military authority, Desdemona's social constraints, and the Senate's institutional power all create a complex web. A single character's language cannot constitute exploration of how power is 'presented' across the text. The response also needs to address what different readers/audiences — Jacobean and contemporary — might make of Shakespeare's construction of these power dynamics."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-174","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's AO1 is consistently praised but they plateau at Band 3. Their responses are well-written and accurate but examiners note: 'clearly knows the text but does not offer a personal, evaluative response.' What are they missing? (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"They need to include more quotations from the text.","B":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","C":"They are meeting AO1 (accurate, well-written expression with terminology) and AO2 (technique analysis) but not AO5 — the requirement for an informed personal response that goes beyond describing what the writer does to evaluating its effectiveness, exploring alternative interpretations, or challenging the text's assumptions. Band 4 requires the student's voice to emerge as a reader who has formed views, not just as an analyst reporting findings.","D":"They should include more biographical context about the author."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"AO5 (personal response / creativity) is the most under-taught assessment objective. Students are often told not to write 'I think' or 'In my opinion' — which they interpret as meaning personal response is penalised. In fact, AO5 requires an evaluative, independent, argued position — but expressed through the analytical voice rather than first-person assertion. Signs of strong AO5: (1) The student weighs alternative interpretations ('while some readers might read X as Y, the structural positioning of Z suggests...'); (2) The student evaluates effectiveness ('the metaphor is particularly effective because...'); (3) The student challenges the text or considers the limits of a reading ('this reading is complicated by...'); (4) The student brings a distinctive angle that could only come from their engagement with the text. AO5 is the difference between a response that any well-trained student could produce and one that shows this student has genuinely thought about this text. Examiner language: 'engaged', 'convincing', 'independent', 'evaluative', 'flair'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which of these sentences demonstrates AO5 most effectively?","options":{"A":"'While the lighthouse functions straightforwardly as a symbol of Woolf's own unresolved grief for her mother, reading it only biographically risks reducing the novel's formal complexity to autobiography — the lighthouse is more productively read as a figure for the impossibility of arrival: what draws us forward always recedes as we approach, which is Woolf's larger claim about desire, completion, and the nature of time itself.'","B":"'Explore' means write as much as possible — length signals exploration.","C":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","D":"'The writer uses symbolism to show the theme of isolation.'"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-176","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student's essay on a poem earns high AO2 marks but their teacher notes: 'You analyse lines in isolation — you need to address form and structure.' What does this feedback mean in practice? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"'Similarly' is too informal for A Level essay writing.","C":"The student should write about the poet's life and why they chose this form.","D":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-COMPARISON-FORM","scaffold":"Form and structure are consistently under-addressed in AO2 responses. Students tend to work line-by-line through quotations (micro-analysis) without stepping back to ask how the poem's overall architecture contributes to meaning. Key form and structure questions: (1) Stanza structure — are stanzas regular or irregular? Does regularity create control/constraint or does fragmentation enact psychological disintegration? (2) Enjambment vs end-stopped lines — does meaning spill across line breaks (creating momentum, connection, breathlessness) or is each line contained (creating finality, separation, emphasis)? (3) Caesura — where does the poem pause mid-line and why? Does the pause enact hesitation, contrast, shock? (4) Volta — where does the poem turn? What does the turn enact? (5) Opening and closing — how does the ending respond to or subvert the opening? (6) Form choices — sonnet, free verse, dramatic monologue — what does the chosen form contribute that another form could not? AO2 mark: micro-analysis without macro-structure = Band 3 ceiling.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student analyses Duffy's 'War Photographer' line by line but doesn't mention that the poem is four regular sestets with an irregular final stanza. Why does this omission matter?","options":{"A":"The student is doing close analysis of individual words and phrases (micro-level AO2) but not addressing how the poem is organised at the macro level — how the stanza structure shapes the argument, how enjambment and caesura control pace and emphasis, where the poem's volta (turn) occurs and what it enacts, how the ending relates to the beginning. Form and structure analysis asks: why is this poem shaped this way, and what does the architecture of the whole contribute to meaning?","B":"The structural observation is analytically significant: the three regular sestets enact the photographer's routine, disciplined work — developing images, cataloguing atrocities — while the final stanza's rhythmic disruption mirrors the breakdown of that professional distance as the images emerge. The form itself enacts the poem's argument: order cannot contain the disorder of war. Missing this means the student has analysed the trees while missing the shape of the forest.","C":"The student should mention the form briefly as a separate paragraph.","D":"Duffy's form choices are less important than her word choices."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-178","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"In a third-person novel, the narrator writes: 'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.' The student writes: 'The narrator tells us the character feels lonely.' Which term and analysis is most precise? (alternative exam formulation) (second formulation)","options":{"A":"This is free indirect discourse — the narrator's voice merges with the character's consciousness without speech marks or reporting verbs, allowing the reader to inhabit the character's perspective while the narrator maintains subtle distance. The rhetorical question ('What was the point, really?') and the colloquial 'supposed' are hallmarks of the technique.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"This is an interior monologue — the character speaks directly to the reader.","D":"This is dramatic monologue — the character reveals themselves through speech."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Free indirect discourse (FID): a technique where the narrator's third-person voice merges with a character's thoughts and speech patterns without using reporting verbs ('she thought') or speech marks. Hallmarks: rhetorical questions, colloquialisms, exclamations, hedging language — stylistic features that belong to the character, not a detached narrator. Effect: creates intimacy and sympathy while maintaining the narrator's ironic distance. Contrast with: (1) stream of consciousness — unstructured, associative, minimal punctuation (Woolf, Joyce); (2) interior monologue — first-person thought, explicit ('I thought...'); (3) dramatic monologue — poetic form, one speaker addressed to a silent listener (Browning). AO2 mark: naming FID + identifying the character's voice within the narrator's syntax + analysing the effect of the merger earns top band.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"'He was perfectly happy, of course he was. Why would he feel guilty? He'd done nothing wrong.' What technique is this and what is its effect?","options":{"A":"Stream of consciousness — the thoughts are unstructured and associative.","B":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","C":"Free indirect discourse — the narrator adopts the character's defensive voice and rhetorical questioning, creating dramatic irony as the reader senses guilt the character denies, and maintaining distance through the third person even as it inhabits his perspective.","D":"Interior monologue — the character speaks directly without a narrator."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-180","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student studying AQA English Language & Literature writes: 'The writer uses deixis to point to things.' Which response shows a more sophisticated understanding? (applied formulation) (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Deixis is the same as anaphora — it refers back to earlier parts of the text.","D":"The context is factually incorrect — Macbeth was written in 1603."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GENG-CONTEXT-REGISTER","scaffold":"Deixis (from Greek: 'pointing'): words whose reference shifts depending on context of utterance — they point to the speech situation rather than carrying fixed meaning. Three types: (1) Personal — I, you, we, they (who is included/excluded?); (2) Spatial — here, there, this, that (where is the reader positioned?); (3) Temporal — now, then, yesterday, soon (what is the temporal viewpoint?). AQA 7707 Lang/Lit specifically requires linguistic frameworks applied to literary texts — deixis is one of the highest-value terms students rarely use. Effect analysis: 'The repeated use of we positions the reader as part of a community the writer is constructing, creating solidarity' or 'the demonstrative this brings the reader into immediate proximity with the described scene, creating intimacy.' Distinguish from anaphora (referring back to a previously mentioned item within the text — cohesive device, not deictic).","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A wartime speech opens: 'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields.' A student notes 'the use of we.' What deictic analysis would earn AO2 marks?","options":{"A":"The personal deixis 'we' is used for emphasis.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"'We' is used to avoid saying 'I', which would seem arrogant.","D":"The personal deixis 'we' includes both speaker and audience in a unified collective, constructing national solidarity and shared obligation — Churchill positions himself not as commander but as fellow combatant, erasing hierarchy at the moment of greatest crisis. Combined with anaphora and polysyndeton, the deictic 'we' becomes the grammatical embodiment of collective resistance."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-182","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student writes: 'The play uses dramatic irony when the character makes a mistake.' Is this correct? (comparison formulation) (another formulation)","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Partially — dramatic irony applies only to tragedy, not comedy.","C":"No — dramatic irony requires the audience to possess knowledge that a character on stage lacks, creating tension or comedy from the gap between what the character believes and what the audience knows. The character's mistake is a consequence that may result from dramatic irony, but the mistake itself is not the irony. The irony is the knowledge gap, not the error.","D":"No — dramatic irony is when two characters misunderstand each other on stage."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GENG-VIEWPOINT-IRONY","scaffold":"Dramatic irony: the audience knows something a character does not, creating a gap between the character's understanding and the audience's knowledge. Three effects: (1) Tension — we know the danger the character walks into; (2) Pathos — we watch helplessly as the character makes a fateful choice; (3) Comedy — we laugh at the character's misplaced confidence. Distinguish from: situational irony (the outcome is the opposite of what was expected, for characters AND audience); verbal irony (saying the opposite of what you mean — irony of statement); cosmic irony (fate working against a character's desires). Common exam error: using 'dramatic irony' as a synonym for 'irony' or 'something ironic happens'. AO2 mark: identify who knows what, when — then analyse what effect the knowledge gap creates at that specific moment in the text.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"In Macbeth, Duncan praises Macbeth's castle as 'pleasant' and a 'seat' worthy of his gratitude — moments before entering to be murdered there. What is the precise effect of the dramatic irony here?","options":{"A":"The dramatic irony creates devastating pathos — the audience, knowing the murder plan, hears Duncan's praise of the very site of his death as an unbearable marker of his innocence. Shakespeare compounds the irony through the imagery of hospitality and sanctuary ('seat', 'pleasant') — the language of welcome and trust frames the site of ultimate betrayal, deepening the horror of Macbeth's violation of the host-guest bond.","B":"Deixis refers to words and phrases whose meaning depends entirely on context — particularly on who is speaking, where, and when. Personal deixis (I/you/we), spatial deixis (here/there/this/that), and temporal deixis (now/then/yesterday) all anchor the reader in a specific position relative to the text. The effect is often to position the reader as insider or outsider — 'we' includes or excludes, 'here' implies presence or distance.","C":"Duncan's words are an example of dramatic irony because he makes a mistake.","D":"The audience finds it funny that Duncan is so trusting."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]},{"id":"ENGLIT-COV-184","spec":"ENG-TECH-1","stem":"A student annotates every instance of 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.' What would a more analytical student write? (second formulation) (revision variant)","options":{"A":"The student should call it 'repetition of a motif' which is more precise.","B":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","C":"The student has identified a technique and quoted accurately but has only paraphrased rather than analysed. To reach Band 4 they need to explore how the metaphor constructs meaning: the specific semantic properties of glass (transparent but sealed — others can see in but she cannot break out), the bell jar's scientific associations (containing and preserving specimens under controlled conditions), and the effect of 'enclosed' — passive construction, suggesting the entrapment happens to her rather than being chosen.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GENG-LEXICAL-SYNTAX","scaffold":"'Repetition for emphasis' is the weakest acceptable AO2 observation — it earns the bottom of the mark band because it names a technique but makes no analytical claim about how it creates meaning. Stronger analysis asks: (1) What type of repetition? Anaphora (same start), epistrophe (same end), anadiplosis (end of one clause = start of next), or lexical repetition across a text? (2) What is the cumulative effect of the pattern — not just 'emphasis' but what specific meaning does the repetition build? (3) How does the repetition interact with the rest of the text's form, structure, and context? AO2 mark scheme guidance: 'identifies technique' = lower band; 'analyses how technique shapes meaning with embedded textual evidence and exploration of effect' = higher band. Examiners consistently note that 'repetition for emphasis' without further analysis is the single most common reason students fall below Band 3.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A poem ends each stanza with a variation of 'and still she waited.' A student writes: 'The repetition emphasises how long she waited.' What would improve this?","options":{"A":"The student should say it is a refrain — a repeated line in poetry.","B":"The student should identify the structural repetition as functioning like an epistrophic refrain — its recurrence at each stanza's close creates a drumbeat of anticipation and stasis. The word 'still' carries dual weight: temporal ('continuing to wait') and physical ('motionless') — the repetition accumulates these meanings across the poem until waiting becomes the character's entire identity, her existence reduced to this single suspended action.","C":"The student should note that repetition is a common poetic technique.","D":"The student should identify whether the repetition is: anaphora (repeated at the start of clauses), epistrophe (repeated at the end), or semantic field membership (lexical repetition building a conceptual cluster). They should then analyse the cumulative effect rather than simply noting the repetition — 'the recurring darkness lexis creates a semantic field of moral and physical obscurity, positioning the Gothic setting as a space where social norms are suspended and danger is ambient rather than located.'"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"aqa-a-englit-3-2","specPointIds":["aqa-a-englit-3-2","aqa-a-engll-3-2"]}]}});
